    "The Ralli Brothers firm, based in London, traded in grain, cotton, silk and later jute from India, where it opened offices in Calcutta and Bombay from 1851; at its height the firm employed more than 40,000 people, yet control stayed within the extended family. By the mid-19th century it was one of the largest trading firms in Britain. The Greeks of London built the great Orthodox church of St Sophia in Bayswater in 1877–1879, consecrated in 1882 and later the cathedral of the Archdiocese of Thyateira and Great Britain. Its foundation stone was laid by Eustratios Ralli, and the community raised the £50,000 it cost within three years. The Greek community of Britain today, much enlarged by Greek Cypriots who came after 1950, numbers in the hundreds of thousands.",
